※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=1
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4600 LANG_DOC_UNKNOWN_COMMAND
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4601 LANG_DOC_PARAM_UNKNOWN
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4602 LANG_DOC_PARAM_TWICE
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4603 LANG_DOC_RETURN_OF_NOTHING
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4604 LANG_DOC_NOT_A_FUNCTION

※ Every way a documentation comment can say something that is not so.
※
※ They are warnings and not errors: what is wrong is the comment, and a comment
※ cannot make a program mean something else.  They are on rather than asked for,
※ because a comment that names a parameter the function has not got is always a
※ mistake -- the parameter was renamed, or the line was copied and not finished --
※ and the reader it misleads is the one who trusted it.
※
※ Each is `-Wno-` able by the name in the catalog: `doc-command`, `doc-param`
※ and `doc-return`.

※※ \param missing there is no parameter of this name
※※ \param n the one there is
※※ \param n and again, which is the same one twice
※※ \wrong what is this
@[visible]
fn one(n: u8) → u8:
    n

※※ \return this one answers with nothing
@[visible, impure]
fn two():
    seen ← 1u8

※※ \param a a type takes nothing
type Pair = a : u8 ; b : u8

let seen: mut u8 = 0u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    two()
    let both: Pair = Pair(.a ← one(seen), .b ← 0u8)
    ⎕narrow(both.a, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
